CAT = "sgresInterests"; SEG = "INT"
Q = [
("A mortgage over a property is best described as:",
 "Security for a loan", ["A transfer of ownership", "A type of tenancy", "A tax on the loan"],
 "A mortgage gives the lender security over the property for repayment. The owner keeps the title, subject to the lender's rights if the loan is not repaid."),
("If a borrower defaults on a registered mortgage, the mortgagee's most common remedy is:",
 "Exercising its power of sale", ["Applying for distress", "Compulsory acquisition", "Lodging another caveat"],
 "On default, the mortgagee can sell the property under its power of sale, after serving the required notice, and apply the proceeds to the debt. Foreclosure is rare in practice."),
("When a buyer uses CPF savings to pay for a home, CPF Board's interest is protected by:",
 "A charge on the property", ["A licence over the unit", "A lease to CPF Board", "A transfer of title to CPF"],
 "CPF Board registers a charge so that the CPF used, with accrued interest, is refunded to the owner's CPF account when the property is sold. The charge ranks behind the mortgage."),
("To give a property to a family member as a gift, the owner must:",
 "Sign and register a transfer", ["Simply hand over the keys and title", "Notify CEA in writing", "Lodge a caveat only"],
 "Legal title passes only when a transfer is registered at SLA. A gift transfer also attracts stamp duty, based on market value."),
("A gift of property made shortly before the donor is made bankrupt may be:",
 "Set aside as an undervalue", ["Protected from any challenge", "Taxed as the donee's income", "Turned into a lease"],
 "Under the Insolvency, Restructuring and Dissolution Act 2018, a transaction at an undervalue before bankruptcy can be set aside so the asset returns to the bankrupt's estate."),
("In a trust of land, the trustee holds the legal title and the beneficiary holds:",
 "The equitable interest", ["No interest at all", "A bare licence", "A registered mortgage"],
 "A trust splits ownership: trustees hold legal title and must manage the property for the beneficiaries, who hold the beneficial (equitable) interest."),
("A buyer of land held on trust should check that:",
 "The trustees have power to sell", ["The beneficiaries pay the stamp duty", "The trust is listed with CEA", "The trustees are all lawyers"],
 "The trust deed or the law must give the trustees power to sell, and all trustees usually need to sign. Otherwise the buyer's title may be challenged by beneficiaries."),
("When an owner dies leaving a valid will, the executor deals with the property after obtaining:",
 "A grant of probate", ["Letters of administration", "A lasting power of attorney", "A caveat over the title"],
 "Probate confirms the executor's authority to administer the estate, and the executor then registers a transmission. Letters of administration are for estates with no will or no executor."),
("When a non-Muslim owner dies without a will, the estate is distributed under:",
 "The Intestate Succession Act", ["Muslim (faraid) law", "The Residential Property Act", "The Estate Agents Act"],
 "The Intestate Succession Act sets the shares for spouses, children and other relatives. An administrator obtains letters of administration to deal with the property."),
("The estate of a Muslim who dies in Singapore is generally distributed under:",
 "Muslim (faraid) law", ["The Intestate Succession Act", "Equal shares to all children", "The Women's Charter"],
 "The Administration of Muslim Law Act applies Syariah rules of inheritance (faraid) to Muslim estates, with fixed shares for heirs. Civil intestacy rules apply to non-Muslims."),
("A grant gives land to A for life, then to B. B's interest is:",
 "A remainder", ["A reversion", "A lease", "A licence"],
 "B holds a future interest called a remainder, which takes effect when A's life estate ends. If the land returned to the grantor instead, the grantor's interest would be a reversion."),
("When one of two joint tenants dies, the deceased's interest:",
 "Passes to the survivor", ["Passes under the will", "Passes to the State", "Is split among the heirs"],
 "Under the right of survivorship, the surviving joint tenant takes the whole, regardless of the deceased's will. Tenants in common, by contrast, hold shares that pass under their estates."),
("Co-owners who want each share to pass under their own wills should hold the property as:",
 "Tenants in common", ["Joint tenants", "Life tenants", "Licensees"],
 "Tenants in common hold distinct shares, possibly unequal, that pass to their estates on death. Joint tenants have no separate shares and survivorship applies."),
("A Singapore-incorporated company buying a commercial shophouse holds the title:",
 "In its own name", ["In a director's name", "Only through a trust", "On a licence from SLA"],
 "A company is a separate legal person and can hold land in its own name. The Residential Property Act restricts foreign companies buying residential property, but not commercial property."),
("An owner has lost mental capacity and made no lasting power of attorney. To sell the property, the family usually needs:",
 "A court-appointed deputy", ["Consent from the neighbours", "An approval letter from CEA", "A grant of probate"],
 "Under the Mental Capacity Act 2008, if there is no LPA, the court can appoint a deputy with power to deal with the person's property. Probate applies only after death."),
("Under the Residential Property Act 1976, a foreign person buying a landed house generally needs:",
 "Prior government approval", ["No approval of any kind", "Only a bank's letter", "Only HDB's consent"],
 "Landed homes are restricted residential property, so a foreign person must obtain approval from SLA's Land Dealings Approval Unit before buying. Condominium units are not restricted."),
("Plot ratio is the ratio of:",
 "Gross floor area to site area", ["Site area to the building's height", "Net lettable to gross area", "Strata area to land area"],
 "Plot ratio caps development intensity: maximum gross floor area equals site area times plot ratio. A 1,000 m2 site with a plot ratio of 2.8 allows 2,800 m2 of GFA."),
("Which plan shows the permissible land use and density for each site in Singapore?",
 "URA's Master Plan", ["SLA's land register", "HDB's sales brochure", "BCA's Green Mark list"],
 "The Master Plan, reviewed every five years, sets the zoning and plot ratio for each site and can be checked through URA Space."),
("Using a property for something other than its approved use generally requires first getting:",
 "Planning permission", ["CEA's written consent", "A new title from SLA", "MAS approval"],
 "A change of use is development under the Planning Act and needs URA's planning permission, and for HDB property HDB's approval. Using premises without it can lead to enforcement action."),
("Under the 60:40 rule for industrial premises, ancillary uses such as offices may take up to:",
 "40% of the floor area", ["60% of the floor area", "10% of the floor area", "Half of the floor area"],
 "At least 60% of the floor area must be used for the predominant industrial use, so ancillary uses like offices and showrooms are capped at 40%."),
]
